<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · river</p><h1 class="enc-title">Gandaki River</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Gandak, Narayani River</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Gandaki, known as the Gandak in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> and the Narayani in its lower course in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nepal">Nepal</a>, is a river that rises in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/himalayas">Himalayas</a> of Nepal and flows south across the plains of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bihar">Bihar</a> to join the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ganges-plain">Ganges</a> near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/patna">Patna</a>. It is often identified with the Sadanira of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shatapatha-brahmana">Shatapatha Brahmana</a>, the river that marked the boundary between Kosala and Videha.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/gandaki-river.jpg" alt="Gandaki River" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: 南アジア · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Narayani_bridge_in_Gaindakot.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
